  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 240 AM EDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nTo start to following work week, we will look to be nestled between \nweak surface ridging over the northern Gulf and southwest Atlantic, \nwhich should allow our winds to be fairly light to perhaps \noccasionally moderate as one could say they will be mainly sea \nbreeze-driven while still maintaining a faint easterly component. \nThis will drastically change beginning Tuesday and lasting through \nmuch of the upcoming week as strong surface ridging sets up over New \nEngland and Atlantic Canada and expands spatially to the south and \nwest to encompass nearly the entire eastern seaboard of the CONUS. \nThe reason as to why this ridge will be having such an impact on \nSouth Florida despite its axis being located over 1500 miles to our \nnortheast is the significant expansion it will undergo and the \nanomalous near-surface pressures that will overspread the eastern \nUnited States. Both NAEFS (combination of GEFS and GEPS members) and \nECMWF ENS (ensemble) forecasts continue to indicate 1000-mb \ngeopotential heights being above the 90th climatological percentile, \nultimately equating in stronger northeasterly winds that will noted \nacross South Florida from Tuesday onward. Aloft, an even stronger \nridge that could be at or near all-time records with respect to 500-\nmb height anomalies will be building over the eastern US. The only \ndifference we are noticing amongst the newest model runs, \nspecifically towards the latter half of next week, is the potential \nfor a pronounced cut-off low that will be separating from the \nmainstream flow of a large upper trough traversing eastern Canada to \ndrop southward and approach South Florida. Previous model runs had \nthe expansive upper-level ridge largely dominating the eastern US \nand even the western Atlantic for that matter, but have since shown \na retrograde to the west. Though this potential outcome could give \nthe aberrant cutoff low a chance to ride the eastern periphery of \nthis upper ridge all the way down towards the southwest Atlantic and \npossibly South Florida, it is too early to know what kind of impact, \nif any, it could have on our local weather.\n\nSaharan dust is another meteorological component we will be \nmonitoring next week as NASA GEOS Dust Extinction Aerosol forecast \nshows a potential intrusion into South Florida as early as Monday \nevening and lasting through at least midweek, though it is important \nto note that the thickest concentrations will be staying to our \nsouth courtesy of the abnormally strong ridging over the eastern US. \nNBM has our regional PWATs dropping to at or below 2\" through \nmidweek, which could act to lower our overall rain chances with more \nuncertainty as to whether some dust will linger or more moisture \nwill enter the region for the second half of the week. With the \nincreasing northeasterly flow we will experience by Tuesday (and \neven on Monday with a light easterly flow in place), expect the \nmajority of showers and thunderstorms to favor the western half of \nSouth Florida during each afternoon cycle (which is indicated in our \ndaily PoPs and backed by Day 1-5 QPFs). Highs temperatures each \nafternoon next week should be reaching the low 90s across our \neastern metros (possibly throwing some upper 80s in there especially \nwhen that stronger northeasterly wind component kicks in) and the \nmid 90s across the interior and SW Florida with hotter feels-like \ntemperatures areawide generally in the 100-105F range. Evenings will \nbe warm and muggy with overnight lows dropping into the low to mid \n70s across the interior and around Lake Okeechobee and into the mid \nto upper 70s across both our coastal metros.",
